/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 211 0 R
/T (A second inter-regional process is related to the Association Agreement between the EU and Mercosur, finalized after two decades of negotiations. Talks began in 2000, but they halted from 2004 to 2010 for various reasons. While methodological disagreements played a role \(Dominguez, 2024\), the political and economic context changed in both regions. On the one side, the EU was in the midst of the enlargement process towards Eastern European states and the failure of the FTAA reduced the competitive pressure with the US that had been driving these negotiations. One the other side, the wave of post-hegemonic regionalism resulted in a deviation from the liberal EU and WTO-plus model of trade agreements \(Alvarez, 2024\). The economies of Mercosur countries were also weakened by the 1998 Brazilian and 2001 Argentinian economic crises which triggered intra-Mercosur conflicts and disagreements in the absence of institutional mechanism to deal with such conflicts \(Mata Diz & Almeida Luquini, 2011; Mattheis & Wunderlich, 2017\). This institutional deficit was part of a broader disappointment for the EU which came to understand that Mercosur was not about to replicate the EU institutional model as it embraced a purely inter-governmental framework \(Mattheis & Wunderlich, 2017; Nolte & Neito, 2021\).)
/E (A second inter-regional process is related to the Association Agreement between the EU and Mercosur, finalized after two decades of negotiations. Talks began in 2000, but they halted from 2004 to 2010 for various reasons. While methodological disagreements played a role \(Dominguez, 2024\), the political and economic context changed in both regions. On the one side, the EU was in the midst of the enlargement process towards Eastern European states and the failure of the FTAA reduced the competitive pressure with the US that had been driving these negotiations. One the other side, the wave of post-hegemonic regionalism resulted in a deviation from the liberal EU and WTO-plus model of trade agreements \(Alvarez, 2024\). The economies of Mercosur countries were also weakened by the 1998 Brazilian and 2001 Argentinian economic crises which triggered intra-Mercosur conflicts and disagreements in the absence of institutional mechanism to deal with such conflicts \(Mata Diz & Almeida Luquini, 2011; Mattheis & Wunderlich, 2017\). This institutional deficit was part of a broader disappointment for the EU which came to understand that Mercosur was not about to replicate the EU institutional model as it embraced a purely inter-governmental framework \(Mattheis & Wunderlich, 2017; Nolte & Neito, 2021\).)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
