/T (Other areas of Asia, including South-, Central- and West Asia, and parts of what is often referred to as the Pacific, are home to more organizations, but their evolution has been less dynamic than the above-mentioned institutions headquartered in East and Southeast Asia. In South Asia, the Indian Ocean Rim Association for Regional Cooperation, established in 1999 and comprising 23 states, works on strengthening economic ties and preventing environmental degradation and crime \(Panke et al., 2022\). )
/E (Other areas of Asia, including South-, Central- and West Asia, and parts of what is often referred to as the Pacific, are home to more organizations, but their evolution has been less dynamic than the above-mentioned institutions headquartered in East and Southeast Asia. In South Asia, the Indian Ocean Rim Association for Regional Cooperation, established in 1999 and comprising 23 states, works on strengthening economic ties and preventing environmental degradation and crime \(Panke et al., 2022\). )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2 3]
>>
endobj
439 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 214 0 R
/T (In the Middle East, maybe more than many other regions in the world, is governed by grievances, historical and contemporary. Those multi-axes grievances shape the politics in the Middle East and also the design and functioning of regional organizations in the region. All countries in the region are, in different ways, marked by these overlapping grievances; between Arabs and Jews, between Hamas and Fatah, between Shia, Sunni, Druze and Maronites. On top of all these domestic grievances are regional conflicts that are weaved into the domestic grievances. Regional powers like Iran, Turkey, Israel, Saudi Arabia, and Qatar try, sometimes successfully, to mobilize the schisms and grievances to their benefit. So, too, do great powers. These powers compete with each other for the support of the different countries and warring factions within the countries, establishing patron-clients with them, providing arms, money, and diplomatic backing. )
/E (In the Middle East, maybe more than many other regions in the world, is governed by grievances, historical and contemporary. Those multi-axes grievances shape the politics in the Middle East and also the design and functioning of regional organizations in the region. All countries in the region are, in different ways, marked by these overlapping grievances; between Arabs and Jews, between Hamas and Fatah, between Shia, Sunni, Druze and Maronites. On top of all these domestic grievances are regional conflicts that are weaved into the domestic grievances. Regional powers like Iran, Turkey, Israel, Saudi Arabia, and Qatar try, sometimes successfully, to mobilize the schisms and grievances to their benefit. So, too, do great powers. These powers compete with each other for the support of the different countries and warring factions within the countries, establishing patron-clients with them, providing arms, money, and diplomatic backing. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
440 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 214 0 R
/T (Because most conflicts involve different sets of grievances, addressing some of them becomes difficult because such efforts are likely to inflate grievances that are not addressed. A case in point is the 2022 maritime border agreement between Israel and Lebanon. Arguably, the agreement settled a thorny issue with the potential of reducing tensions, even paving the road for resolution of many of the other conflicts between Israel and Lebanon. But in a series of political maneuvering that mobilized grievances, Netanyahu succeeded in portraying the agreement as an unforgivable compromise giving up economic and security national interests, and even as a surrender to the Hezbollah. And in Lebanon a mirror image developed, ensuring that the agreement would not be a springboard for peace, but rather an open wound paving the way for increased tensions and more violence. All conflicts in the Middle East are similar in that regard, involving multi-axes grievances. )
/E (Because most conflicts involve different sets of grievances, addressing some of them becomes difficult because such efforts are likely to inflate grievances that are not addressed. A case in point is the 2022 maritime border agreement between Israel and Lebanon. Arguably, the agreement settled a thorny issue with the potential of reducing tensions, even paving the road for resolution of many of the other conflicts between Israel and Lebanon. But in a series of political maneuvering that mobilized grievances, Netanyahu succeeded in portraying the agreement as an unforgivable compromise giving up economic and security national interests, and even as a surrender to the Hezbollah. And in Lebanon a mirror image developed, ensuring that the agreement would not be a springboard for peace, but rather an open wound paving the way for increased tensions and more violence. All conflicts in the Middle East are similar in that regard, involving multi-axes grievances. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
